"Build, end to end, a physical alarm panel using my GitHub\nUniverse 2025 badge (Pimoroni Tufty 2350, RP2350, MonaOS, MicroPython)\nand my Verisure alarm. The badge shows the current alarm status, and\neach badge button switches the alarm to a specific mode. Deliver every\ncomponent, not just the badge app.\n\nCOMPONENTS TO DESIGN AND BUILD\n1. Verisure adapter: logs in through an unofficial Verisure API, reads\n   the alarm state, lists the modes my account supports, and sets a\n   mode. Evaluate existing community projects first (the \"My Verisure\"\n   GraphQL integration and the Securitas Direct / Verisure EU\n   component and CLI) and reuse one if it fits. I'm in Brazil, and\n   those projects are only reported to work there, not confirmed.\n2. Bridge service: authenticated \"status\" and \"set mode\" endpoints\n   that the badge calls, backed by the adapter. Compare where it can\n   run: my phone (Termux or Tasker on Android, Shortcuts on iPhone),\n   Home Assistant, or a small self-hosted service. Recommend one and\n   build it.\n3. Badge app: a MicroPython app in apps/<name>/__init__.py.\n   - Main screen shows the current mode (large, readable at a\n     glance), when it was last updated, and connection health. The\n     backlight colour follows the mode.\n   - Refreshes automatically at a sensible interval and right after a\n     mode change.\n   - Each of the buttons A, B, C, UP and DOWN is mapped to one mode,\n     including disarmed. The mapping lives in a config file.\n   - After pressing a button, the screen shows sending, then the\n     confirmed new mode read back from the alarm (not just \"sent\"),\n     or a clear error. If the bridge is unreachable, show the last\n     known state marked as stale.\n4. Config and secrets handling, tests, a simulator run, deployment\n   scripts, and a README covering setup, flashing the badge, running\n   the bridge, and security notes.\n\nCONSTRAINTS\n- Stock MonaOS, normal app, no custom firmware. Check the official\n  example apps in github.com/badger/home for the real WiFi/HTTP/button\n  API before writing code. Don't invent function names.\n- The badge stores only the bridge URL and a shared token. Verisure\n  credentials and any alarm code exist only on the bridge.\n- Secrets in git-ignored config or environment variables, never in\n  the repo or the logs.\n- The badge stays in my room, so I accept physical-theft risk. The\n  bridge is still a network endpoint, so keep token auth, HTTPS or\n  LAN-only access, rate limiting, and no direct exposure to the public\n  internet.\n- The unofficial API may break, so keep it behind the adapter and make\n  failures visible on the badge instead of silent.\n- Do the Verisure adapter first as a spike. Gate: prove I can read the\n  state and change modes on my real alarm through the API before\n  building the rest. If the gate fails, stop and propose alternatives.\n\nNON-GOALS\n- No TV/IR remote, no on-device AI, no other badge features.\n- No alarm history, camera, or lock features in this version.\n\nDECISIONS TO ASK ME\n- Phone platform and where the bridge runs.\n- Which modes my account has, and the button-to-mode mapping.\n- Whether my account needs a PIN to change modes.\n- Whether disarming should require holding the button, to avoid\n  accidental presses.\n- Whether to use a lighter workflow, since this is a personal project.\n\nDONE WHEN\n- The spike proves status read and mode change on my real alarm.\n- The badge shows the live mode, and each button switches to its mode\n  end to end with the confirmed state displayed.\n- Failure cases (no WiFi, bridge down, bad token, Verisure error) show\n  clear messages and a stale-state marker.\n- Tests pass and the README lets me rebuild everything from scratch."
